Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"AI alignment suffers from two interconnected problems of overfitting":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An AI agent essentially must optimize for two objectives. The overall objective, given to it by its creator, and also the mesa objective that is undermined as a means to achieve the overall objective. However, overfitting can occur in both these instances, and thus lead to a failure of optimization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes in detail the two related problems of optimization in AI alignment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The agent is given an overall objective that it optimizes for, it then generates a mesa objective which it must more directly optimize for in order to optimize the overall objective. This, however, means that there are two potential nodes where overfitting occurs, and thus makes the AI optimization problem even more difficult, since both need to avoid overfitting in order for the agent to be properly aligned.</p><p>\\[insert diagram here]</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Misaligned systems persist due to competition with misaligned incentives\">Misaligned systems persist due to competition with misaligned incentives</a><ul><li>This is in a similar vein, though there is no competition, but rather just direct optimization.</li></ul></li></ul>","Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A consumption tax can be made progressive if it is rebated in a progressive manner. Usually, consumption taxes are regressive, since low-income people have a higher MPC, meaning that they would proportionally be taxed more. This can be ameliorated with a progressive rebate system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds complexity to the idea that consumption taxes are totally regressive, since they could be made progressive with the right rebate system.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A revenue neutral carbon tax could be tax at, for example, 10%. With this in place, a low-income individual pays $100 in taxes, while a wealthier people might pay $1000. After taxes are levied, each individual receives $500. This makes the tax progressive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of the tax system in the US, related the content of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of the US tax system, and how expenditures are often hidden through tax breaks.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cells are typically negatively charged":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the fact that many of the molecules in a cell are negatively charged, most prominently Potassium ions, cells are typically negatively on the whole. They are usually around $-60$ to $-70 mV$, known as its <em>resting potential</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This important for explaining the movement of molecules across the cell membrane, and the membrane potential.</p><h4>References</h4>","Chinese firms' global market shares have increased largely due to subsidies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A large reason for the increase in Chinese firms’ global market shares is due to the large amounts of subsidies that they get from the government, which accounts for around 10% of a firm’s annual revenue. This is compared to the 2% OECD average.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the large amount of market share which Chinese companies occupy in many industrial sectors.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Much of the subsidies were given in the form of “soft loans,” or below-market rate loans from the government, and which are hard to trace. Additionally, this strategy does not increase the productivity of the Chinese firms, but simply makes them unfair competitors compared to other OECD firms.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>For instance, economic domination.</li></ul></li></ul>","Credit expansion ends in specie outflow, hoarding, and unemployment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An issue of money beyond what productivity supports raises consumption and import demand, foreign sellers refuse the paper and are paid in metal, the outflow destroys the backing for the paper, holders of capital hoard what metal remains, and manufacturers dismiss workers for want of liquidity until falling incomes kill the import demand and the drain stops.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The sequence is a complete account of a monetary cycle written before the analytical apparatus for one existed, and it identifies the balance of payments as the binding constraint on domestic credit.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Gervaise sets it out as a cycle with a definite terminus: equilibrium is restored not by policy but by the contraction itself. The unemployment is a consequence of hoarding rather than of the price adjustment, which is the part that anticipates later work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity\">A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity</a><ul><li>The constraint that drives the first stage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy\">Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy</a><ul><li>The mechanism of the later stages.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank\">Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank</a><ul><li>The modern version of the same causal story.</li></ul></li></ul>","Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>Democracy and constitutional liberalism are not the same thing, and can be unlinked from each other.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>Understanding this helps us better analyze the situation present in many nations, where there may be the lack of one or another, but often, we may just say that the country lacks “democracy.”</p><h4>The Difference</h4><p>Democracy refers to the process by which leaders are election, namely, through a free and open election. While this would entail some necessary freedoms, such as freedom of the press and association, it does not require a lot. Constitutional Liberalism refers to the existence of legal-political institutions that are “constitutional” and “liberal.” This would include things like the protection of property rights, the enshrinment of certain political rights such as speech, gun ownership, or religion, or <em>habeus corpus</em> and the rule of law.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Many today are forgetting the important of constitutional liberalism and the importance of process, and I think that this is partly because people are conflating democracy and constitutional liberalism and especially a disregard for the latter. Without the rule of law, the state does suffering from a lack of information and transparency that eventually puts it behind the nations that have rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>An explication of the contents in this note which puts it into practice.</li></ul></li></ul>","East of Eden":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A sweeping family saga set in California’s Salinas Valley that follows the intertwined lives of the Trask and Hamilton families across generations. At its center are the Trask brothers, Adam and Charles, and later Adam’s twin sons, Aron and Cal, whose struggle with jealousy, love, guilt, and the search for identity echoes the biblical story of Cain and Abel. The novel explores the idea of <em>timshel</em>—the belief that people are not doomed by fate or sin but have the power to choose between good and evil.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is considered to be Steinbeck’s best work, and one of the best American novels ever written.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral mastery is achieved through choice\">Moral mastery is achieved through choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Man is created in the image of God\">Man is created in the image of God</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People sin so that they may be loved\">People sin so that they may be loved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is the root cause of immorality\">Lying is the root cause of immorality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One&#x27;s fate is not determined by their birth\">One's fate is not determined by their birth</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Cyrus Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Intelligent, but rash and obstinate. A liar (he lied about his experiences as a soldier) (19).<ul><li>He falls entirely under his own lie, and by living his lie, he creates a life for himself.<ul><li>It is interesting how although he himself was a terrible soldier, he creates this illusion of being a great soldier around him, and he idealizes soldiers as honorable people (24).</li><li>In fact, he forces his children to live under this lie with him.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He has an obsession with discipline.<ul><li>He has a unique sense of justice that seems strict and idiosyncratic.<ul><li>For instance, he seems to love Adam more, since his mother killed herself, and he possibly never forgave himself for her death, and felt that he owed Adam.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>His knowledge/understanding of the world is entirely built upon his understanding of military strategy? (26)<ul><li>He also says how one can drive someone too far, and thus must always leave a way out. This is reminiscent of military strategy.</li><li>“If you can endure the worst of life, you can experience the best of it.”</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Charles Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Disciplined and violent.<ul><li>He seems like a man of contradictions.</li><li>He beats up Adam when they are young.<ul><li>It could be that he beats him up out of love? He is trying to get Adam to fight back, but he never does, and that is why he keeps on beating him up. <ul><li>To get Adam to fight back would mean that he conquered his fear, and thus his father would no longer love him as much?</li><li>This would “equalize” them in Cyrus’ eyes, and perhaps that would make Charles feel better?</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He loves Cyrus, and does not like Adam because Cyrus only loves Adam and not him.<ul><li>However, Cyrus does not love him back, and only seems to love Adam. This is why Charles has such enmity against Adam in the book.<ul><li>Cyrus days that Charles has no fears (and therefore no honor?) and therefore he does not love Charles. But this is not true. <ul><li>Ironically, it is clear that Charles fears not being loved by Cyrus (36).</li><li>It is also stated that he fears women (45).</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Charles seems like a man defeated by the world. He offers no resistance to it. Perhaps this is because everything he does is in an effort to win the respect of his father, but he no longer gets the chance to do this after his father is gone?</li><li>Charles warms up to Adam after he learns about him escaping prison.<ul><li>Perhaps he thinks that Adam has finally “earned” his love from Cyrus. </li><li>Perhaps he thinks that Adam is no longer perfect, and thus would no longer earn the love of Cyrus, and so they are essentially on equal footing.</li></ul></li><li>He is afraid that the money that Cyrus got was illegitimate since that would undermine his perfect conception of his father in his head. This is perhaps why he is always stressing out about the money.</li></ul></li><li>He is able to see through Cathy, where no one else has been able to.</li><li>He is like a combination of Ivan Karamazov and Dimitri Karamazov. He is cold, but at times impulsive and emotional. He embraces the base aspects of the world, but is not entirely caught up in it, since he manages it against his noble side as well.</li><li>Charles’ living alone is akin to him having killed Abel. It is also during this time that he gets his own “Mark of Cain.” (66)</li></ul><p><strong>Adam Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Aimless and weak-willed, but good hearted.<ul><li>He is like Alyosha.<ul><li>He tries to suffer for the sin of others (Cathy)? At least he falls in love with someone with sexually deviant tendencies, much like Alyosha does with Lisa.</li></ul></li><li>He is aimless in life, and seems to simply just wander around.<ul><li>He joins the army, and falls into its routine, even though he hates killing people.</li><li>He rejoins the army because he missed the routine that the army brought him.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He hates Cyrus, though he has faith in him.<ul><li>He hates Cyrus because Cyrus forces him to join the army.</li><li>He has faith in Cyrus perhaps because he has no love for him. He simply understands Cyrus as an authority figure (70). </li></ul></li><li>His experience escaping the prison seems to have changed him, as after that point, he begins to have more goals and aspirations, rather than simply going where life takes him.<ul><li>He is more confrontational with Charles, and he decides to go to California.</li><li>He marries Cathy, but pisses off Charles. He marries Cathy because:<ul><li>Cathy is attractive.</li><li>He felt bad for her.</li><li>He feels a connection with her (he was also beaten up).</li><li>Cathy manipulates him into believing that she loved him.</li></ul></li><li>Adam projects who he wants Cathy to be onto the actual Cathy, and fails to see what lies beneath this false facade (262).<ul><li>Biblical reference: Eve is created from Adam’s rib, but she is also the one that leads him to sin.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Adam never really redeems himself?<ul><li>He continued to live in lies, first the lie from Cyrus, then the lie from Cathy, then the lying to Aron about Cathy. </li><li>It is the lying to Aron that would eventually (though indirectly) cause his death. </li><li>Perhaps he was able to redeem himself on his deathbed?</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Cathy Ames</strong></p><ul><li>Literally the devil incarnate?<ul><li><s>She is a little like Fyodor Pavlovitch.</s> She is far more like Ivan.</li><li>Emotionless and focused on her goals.<ul><li>She shoots Adam not to kill him, but merely because he stood in her way.</li></ul></li><li>She is unable to see/believe the goodness of others, because she herself is evil.<ul><li>Perhaps the reason why her meeting with Adam shook her so much is because she never thought that Adam would do something so good for her. This forced her to leave her ability to isolate herself from the “chain of goodness” that Adam connected to her.<ul><li>This chain started from Sam?</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Sexually deviant, evil, manipulative, and attractive (<em>femme fatale?</em>).<ul><li>Enters the employ of Mr. Edwards to take advantage of him.</li><li>Kills her own family in an act of arson because she dislikes them.</li><li>Marries Adam to take advantage of him.</li></ul></li><li>Feels a connection to Charles?<ul><li>Perhaps deep down, they are the same. Charles just has an aspect of his soul advocating for the noble, repressing his base.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Samuel Hamilton</strong></p><ul><li>Morally righteous, smart, warm, and kind. </li><li>He is the opposite of Cathy, and whereas Adam is also the opposite of Cathy in that he is lethargic and directionless, Sam is merely opposite in his motives.</li><li>He is bad a making good, though he is a good person.</li><li>He is an immigrant from Ireland, and this gives him an outside perspective on things, and allows him to see things for effectively.<ul><li>He believes in nurture far more than nature. He thinks that people can change, it is the upbringing and the people around then that results in the character of a person, not solely their inherent nature.<ul><li>But would Cathy disprove this? She seems to be incorrigible.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Lee</strong></p><ul><li>Wise, intelligent, and contained.</li><li>He puts on a facade so that others won’t realize his actual power and knowledge and abilities.</li><li>He is pragmatic.</li></ul><h4>The Naming</h4><ul><li>Samuel, in his talking about mediocrity and greatness, thinks that being great means taking on responsibility and initiative in your life, but it also means the greatest sense of loneliness.<ul><li>This loneliness could come from the fact that you are individually burdened with responsibility for others and you are essentially suffering for the sins of others.</li></ul></li><li>But at the same time, he acknowledges that there may be benefits to being great.<ul><li>He considers himself as little selfish that he is placing his son Tom onto a path of greatness, which may bring great rewards, but is also isolating.</li></ul></li><li>This is all essentially to warn Adam about the consequences of his lackluster lifestyle. He presents Adam with the choice between taking action and becoming great or simply resigning.</li></ul><p><em>Guilt</em></p><ul><li>Guilt is used as a means of excusing ourselves from responsibility because it connects us with people in the past, all the way back to Cain, who have sinned.</li></ul><p><em>Cain and Abel</em></p><ul><li>“Reject is the thing that he fears.”</li><li>Directly connects to the tale between Charles and Adam.<ul><li>It is a template of a universal story, something that occurs generation after generation.<ul><li><strong>Is every story a story of Cain and Abel, a story of rejection and fear and anger?</strong></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Similar to the idea of sin being hell from TBK. We fear confession because we fear rejection by others. <ul><li>TBK offers a solution for this.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a><ul><li>This book explores many themes that are explored in <em>TBK</em> as well.</li></ul></li></ul>","Excellencies are positive externalities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Excellencies are essentially an individual’s traits which have broader benefits for the society at large, rather than just benefiting the individual themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>That excellencies are public goods means that we should try to promote them in society. It also has implications for concepts such as shame.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Excellencies are essentially virtues in Rawls’ view, something that is done that is beyond the requirements of conventional morality that is self-centered.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Shame can be natural or moral\">Shame can be natural or moral</a><ul><li>The implications of this for shame.</li></ul></li></ul>","Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Regarding when we should make friends, Aristotle says that it is more important that we have friends when encountering adversity, since grief is lighted by friends. At the same time, it is more honorable to have friends when you are prospering.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle outlines the duty of friends here.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>We should try not to be open about our grief with our friends, since that would cause them pain. However, we should share our moments of triumph with friends, so that we can experience that together.</li><li>That being said, we can call our friends to aid when in times of need, and when it least disrupts them. We should aid our friends when we notice them suffering.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a><ul><li>Being friends is doing the things that are outlined in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>Only virtuous friendships are able to achieve the things laid out in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","If a superintelligence is able to rewrite its own utility function, it would be neutralized as a threat to humanity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If a superintelligence is able to rewrite its own utility function such that it can reach its own bliss point, there would simply be no reason for such a being to endlessly consume resources and thus eliminates it as a threat to human survival.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents a solution to the problem of AI superintelligence that is typically not considered.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that human societies have already moved in a similar direction. Rather than endlessly increasing our consumption of physical resources, we’ve shifted more towards services. Additionally, if AI is able to achieve a bliss state, it would mean that it has violated local nonsatiation. By designing AI in this manner, we will be able to mitigate the possibility that AI endlessly consumes resources that humans need.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that if we design AIs such that they attempt to satisfy their goals doing as little as possible, their utility functions would be designed such that it is as easy as possible to satisfy. Therefore, it will ensure that AI does not compete with humanity for the same resources.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>It could be the case that designing an AI with capped capabilities as described here could prevent it from successfully manifesting its purpose to the fullest extent?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>This note provides a reason why perhaps they should not be afraid of AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All stable and successful institutions in history have had robust self-correcting institutions such that it is able to correct itself without the need for external stimuli.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core, identifying factor of successful institutions, which may help us understand why existing institutions work and design better institutions for the future.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Per Claude Opus 5:</p><ul><li>British Reform Acts of 1832, 1867, and 1884. Acemoglu and Robinson read these as elites extending the franchise under threat of revolution, because a promise to redistribute while keeping power was not credible. The 1832 Act raised the electorate from roughly 478,000 out of 24 million to 813,000, and the decades after saw radical social reform and a falling Gini in England and Wales. Pressure converted into rule change rather than rupture.</li><li>Westminster removing sitting prime ministers mid-term (Thatcher 1990, Truss 2022) without a regime crisis.</li><li>US 17th Amendment (1913) responding to legislature-appointed Senate corruption; the 13th–15th after 1865.</li><li>German Basic Law (1949) explicitly engineered against Weimar's failure modes: constructive vote of no confidence, 5% threshold, Constitutional Court.</li><li>Post-Watergate: FEC, FOIA amendments, Church Committee.</li><li>Pecora hearings into Glass-Steagall (1933).</li><li>Ostrom's design principles for long-enduring commons institutions include graduated sanctions, rapid access to low-cost local arenas for conflict resolution, and nested enterprises, with monitors who are accountable to users or are the users themselves. Japanese irrigation associations are the standard case.</li><li>Swiss cantons plus the referendum; US federalism. Errors are localized and cheap to reverse.</li></ul><h4>Homeostasis</h4><p>A contrast can be drawn between good self-corrections and the idea of homeostasis in biology. An instruction can be analogized to be a single person, where our internal system is able to self-correct only with the need for external stimuli. On the other hand, good institutions are ones that are able to self-correct without the need for external stimuli, mostly because such external stimuli is almost always negative and harmful for the overall stability of the institution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is a specific example of the broader pattern identified in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Good institutions don’t do this? Perhaps self-correction allows the flaw to be minimized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Important for the design of good institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps whether an institution has self-correcting mechanisms can affect the level of trust in that institution.</li></ul></li></ul>","It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>With Rawls’ A Theory of Justice and Mill’s works as examples, while it seems that both attempt to construct a moral theory which is able to exist in a liberal political framework, though they both fail. Therefore, it seems that it is just impossible to construct a universal moral theory for liberalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this is true, then people should stop attempting to do so.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The moral theories proposed in <em>A Theory of Justice</em> and Mill’s works, such as <em>Utilitarianism</em> or <em>On Liberty</em> seems to have unsolvable flaws which undermine the very nature of the frameworks. The fact that they have both failed seems to suggest that it is simply impossible to construct a moral framework for liberalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This work is mentioned here as an example for the impossibility of constructing a moral theory for liberalism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meritocracy is grounded in autonomy, since it treats persons as agents whose acts make a difference":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Allocating by merit considers only what a person has done and can do, which is to treat them as an agent whose purposeful action changes the world, and this respect for agency is a deontological rather than an instrumental ground for meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the best version of the categorical defence, since it does not depend on merit-based allocation producing good outcomes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>The distinction this claim sits on the categorical side of.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our identity is defined by our nature as agents\">Our identity is defined by our nature as agents</a><ul><li>The general account of agency that this defence draws on.</li></ul></li></ul>","Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mexican immigration to the US is different from immigration from other parts of the globe to the US for a few reasons:</p><ul><li>There only a small geographic distance between the two countries, making it easy for immigration to (continue to) occur, legally and illegally.</li><li>Mexican immigrants show a particular aversion to assimilation to local culture.</li><li>Because many part of the US used to be Mexican, and thus have strong cultural roots to Mexico, Mexican immigration to the US could potentially arouse revanchist sentiments.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a few of the complications regarding Mexican immigration into the US.</p><h4>References</h4>","Pleasure is not a process":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure is not a process, or something that brings us from one state to another.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding the refutations against the claim that pleasure is bad.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>It is complete at every moment that it is experienced. </li><li>A process has steps that it is divided into but you cannot divide pleasure. </li><li>Pleasure does not take time, while processes take time.</li></ul><p>This contributes to the argument that pleasure is not something that is merely for returning to an equilibrium state, or many other arguments that frame pleasure as a process, and therefore, not something that is good or worthy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>This being said, pleasure is NOT the Good.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls’ claim that the agents in the original position would be risk-averse, since they don’t know whether their future selves would want the primary goods, or the amount that they would want. Therefore, they would be conservative and ensure that everyone has a sufficient amount of primary goods rather than being risk-neutral and risk that some people don’t have access to primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the nature/behavior of rational agents from behind the veil of ignorance.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This would essentially mean the adoption of a maximin strategy. Rawls argues that this is rational since we do not know the probability distribution of our future circumstances, so we can’t adopt a utilitarian view towards this can calculate the expected value. Also, only the minimum amount of primary goods is required to satisfy the rational agent’s rational, long-term plan.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The result of accepting this is that people are risk-averse, it seems.</li></ul></li></ul>","Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Small transgressions of the rule of law are the most dangerous since they are the ones that people typically care very little about, though they can effectively serve as precedence for bigger transgressions of the rule of law.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is why is it bad that there should be any transgressions of the rule of law, and that any transgressions should be punished harshly.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Small violations of the rule of law are often easily justified and often not noticed, though such instances can both serve to embolden the transgressor and desensitize outsiders from such activities, eventually leading to the end of rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree\">Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree</a><ul><li>Since this is true, we would want to preserve the rule of law through preventing any transgressions.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The conquests of the Rashidun were made possible by the fact that the geography of the area that they conquered was similar to the geography of the area in which they originated. Additionally, implemented a unique tax system that incentivized people to become Islamic, which both served to raise tax revenues and gain converts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the rapid rise of the Rashidun state.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Some other factors that allowed for their rapid conquest include the weakness of the surrounding states, the Byzantines and Sassanids having recently fought the “Last Great War of Antiquity.”</p><h4>References</h4>","The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the military power of a state is split among different agents who are beyond the control of the central authority, the state is showing a sign of weakness, and often imminent collapse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a pattern that has been observed across time and societies, and can be used as a an indicator for the health of a state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders\">The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a></li><li>[[]]</li></ul>","The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Intellect or intuition (<em>nous</em>) deals with the ability to grasp first principles, or the “extremes” that deductive reasoning cannot reach. It operates in two distinct directions:</p><ul><li>Theoretical <em>nous</em> grasps unchangeable first principles (axioms) through induction. </li><li>Practical <em>nous</em> grasps the immediate, variable facts of a specific situation.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>Intellect also constitute a part of the virtue of wisdom. It is also the foundation upon which scientific knowledge is able to build.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>The complementary intellectual virtue. Intellect grasps the starting points; scientific knowledge reasons from them.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a><ul><li>Intellect constitute the second half of wisdom.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>Intellect is required in order to under the particulars that eventually develop prudence in someone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Practical <em>nous</em> requires being morally virtuous.</li></ul></li></ul>","Utilitarianism fails":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Utilitarianism as an ethical theory fails due to a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Utilitarianism is one of the foremost ethical theories.</p><h4>Arguments</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism\">A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism</a></li></ul><h5>Rawlsian Objections</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Rawls’ book is a major counterargument to utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"On Liberty\">On Liberty</a><ul><li>Mill is a defender of utilitarianism, which is implicitly used in this book.</li></ul></li></ul>","We find something funny because it grants us relief":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We often find things funny because they allow us to release some kind of pent up stress.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could serve as a theory to explain why we laugh.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A soldier facing execution makes a joke about how terrible their last meal is, releasing the pent up tension that exists at the moment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority\">We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>Yet another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
